[b]v0.3.2:[/b]
[h1]Flavorium Universalis - Balance - Informal Administration[/h1]

[i]Compatible with EU5 1.3.* | Requires: Flavorium Universalis[/i]

[hr][/hr]

[h2]New: Informal Administration[/h2]

A government that runs on household and personal ties rather than salaried offices costs less to maintain. Every realm now starts with a 30% discount on bureaucracy upkeep, and each of the six vanilla government-size advances takes 5% of it back, one per age.

[list]
[*] [b]Researching the whole spine costs you the discount.[/b] A realm that takes every government-size advance pays full vanilla upkeep by the Age of Revolutions.
[*] [b]Staying informal keeps it.[/b] A realm that never expands its state administration keeps the saving for the whole game, which is the trade being offered.
[*] [b]Safe on existing saves, in both directions.[/b] Advance modifiers are read from the definition on load and the discount is recomputed every load, so nothing is stored in the save.
[/list]

[h2]Fixes[/h2]

[list]
[*] [b]Bog iron smelters produced nothing.[/b] This submod cuts iron from 3 to 0.8 and leaves coal at its vanilla price, which inverted the one common building that spends coal to make iron: it earned 0.24 against 0.75 of fuel and so never employed anyone. Its coal input is now scaled to match the iron price, restoring roughly the margin vanilla gave it. The iron and tools prices themselves are unchanged.
[/list]

[b]v0.3.1:[/b]
[h1]Flavorium Universalis - Balance - French, German and Spanish[/h1]

[i]Compatible with EU5 1.3.* | Requires: Flavorium Universalis[/i]

[hr][/hr]

[h2]New: French, German and Spanish localization[/h2]

The caste and examination system text is now translated into French, German and Spanish, matching the languages added to the base mod in v0.3.10.

[list]
[*] [b]Machine translated:[/b] produced with a local language model and validated automatically, not reviewed by a native speaker.
[*] [b]No gameplay changes:[/b] this release adds localization files only. English text and all balance values are untouched.
[/list]

[b]v0.3.0:[/b]
[h1]Flavorium Universalis — Balance — Navy, Caste & Examination Systems[/h1]

[i]Compatible with EU5 1.3.* | Requires: Flavorium Universalis[/i]

[hr][/hr]

[h2]Navy Rebalance[/h2]

Ship costs no longer balloon across the ages. The flat gold price of each hull is unchanged — the runaway expense came from the [i]market goods[/i] each ship demanded to build and maintain, which ramped steeply and spiked hard in the Absolutism age. Those curves are now smoothed.

[b]Light & transport ships[/b]
[list]
[*] Build-material cost grows by gentle, consistent increments instead of compounding — the Age-5 spike is gone (e.g. light-ship lumber [b]4 → 6 → 8 → 11 → 14 → 18[/b] instead of vanilla's …10 → [b]16[/b] → 20).
[*] Late-game hulls now cost roughly 5× their Age-1 material bill rather than ~10× with a sudden jump.
[/list]

[b]Heavy ships & galleys[/b]
[list]
[*] Rescaled — still clearly the expensive ships (a Revolutions-age heavy still costs several times a light ship), but the exponential Age-5 jump is flattened (heavy lumber [b]30 → 50[/b] becomes [b]33 → 41 → 50[/b]).
[/list]

[b]Maintenance (all four hull types)[/b]
[list]
[*] Upkeep goods reduced and de-spiked across the board (e.g. light-ship naval supplies upkeep [b]0.5 → 0.28[/b] at Age 6; heavy [b]1.0 → 0.68[/b]), with a clean linear curve on consumables. Heavy ships remain the priciest to keep afloat.
[/list]

[b]Unique flagships[/b]
[list]
[*] [b]Ship of the Line[/b] rescaled to track the lowered heavy curve (lumber [b]120 → 100[/b], cannons [b]30 → 22[/b]) so it stays the premier capital ship without being absurd.
[*] [b]Cakradonya[/b] lightly trimmed to remain a clear premium over standard galleys.
[/list]

[hr][/hr]

[h2]India — The Caste Order[/h2]

A social-mobility system for Indian (dharmic) realms, built on hereditary caste privileges and a reform path.

[list]
[*] [b]Brahmin Ritual Primacy[/b], [b]Kshatriya Martial Order[/b], [b]Jati Occupational Order[/b] — caste privileges that grant order, martial steadiness and revenue at the cost of frozen social mobility.
[*] [b]The Varna Order[/b] government reform codifies the hierarchy for an extra avenue of rule, trading away mobility and administrative flexibility.
[*] [b]Reform the Caste Order[/b] event — loosen the varna order gradually (sanskritisation), abolish caste outright, or uphold it. Reform triggers a [b]Caste Upheaval[/b] period (rapid pop promotion plus unrest) settling into a [b]Reformed Social Order[/b].
[/list]

[hr][/hr]

[h2]China — The Imperial Examinations[/h2]

A bureaucratic-advancement system for realms using the keju (civil-service examinations).

[list]
[*] [b]Scholar-Gentry Examinations[/b] and [b]Examination Lineages[/b] privileges — an able, studious administration whose narrow, costly path keeps office in gentry hands.
[*] [b]The Imperial Examinations[/b] government reform makes the keju the spine of the state for an extra avenue of rule, throttling mobility and making officials slow and costly to recruit.
[*] [b]Reform the Examinations[/b] event — broaden the curriculum, abolish the exams for an open path to office, or keep them. Reform triggers an [b]Examination Upheaval[/b] (dramatic opening plus unrest) settling into a [b]Reformed Bureaucracy[/b].
[/list]

[hr][/hr]

[b]v0.1.0:[/b]
[h1]Flavorium Universalis — Balance — Initial Release[/h1]

[i]Compatible with EU5 1.2.* | Requires: Flavorium Universalis[/i]

[hr][/hr]

Optional re-balance submod extracted from the main Flavorium Universalis mod. Subscribe to this alongside the base mod if you want these balance tweaks; skip it to play with vanilla culture capacity and literacy values.

[hr][/hr]

[h2]Culture Capacity[/h2]

Vanilla culture capacity bonuses are broadly reduced to make cultural integration a meaningful constraint rather than a free expansion tool.

[b]Advances (×4 reduced)[/b]
[list]
[*] Cultural Acceptance, Vibrant Court, Persian in the Court, Millets — [b]1.0 → 0.5[/b] cultures capacity
[/list]

[b]Government Reforms (×8 reduced)[/b]
[list]
[*] French Feudal Nobility, Legacy of the Ottomans, Mughal Diwan, Legacy of Kublai Khan, Bohemian Commonwealth, Mandala System — reduced from +3 to +1 or +2
[*] Iroquois Reforged Law of Peace — reduced from +2 to +1
[*] Partitio Reform, Restored Rule of the Latins — reduced to +1 or +2
[/list]

[b]Laws[/b]
[list]
[*] Allow Foreign Rituals (Foreign Cultural Law category) — changed from flat [b]+1 cultures capacity[/b] to [b]+20% cultures capacity modifier[/b]
[/list]

[hr][/hr]

[h2]Literacy[/h2]

Literacy is class-stratified — upper classes (nobles, clergy, burghers) have more avenues to high literacy than peasants, soldiers, and laborers. Global max literacy is reduced to make the Library and scholarly cabinet ministers more impactful.

[b]Advance overrides (×3)[/b]
[list]
[*] Written Alphabet — [b]global_max_literacy 5 → 2[/b]
[*] Printing Press — replaces [b]global +10[/b] with [b]+5 nobles / +5 clergy / +5 burghers[/b]
[*] Innovativeness — replaces [b]global +5[/b] with [b]+5 nobles / +5 clergy / +5 burghers[/b]
[/list]

[b]Library building override[/b]
[list]
[*] Replaces vanilla [b]local_max_literacy +5[/b] with class-split bonuses: +3 nobles, +3 clergy, +2 burghers, +0.03/month literacy
[/list]

[b]Regional catch-up advances (×4 new)[/b]
Partial offsets for societies with strong literacy traditions:
[list]
[*] [b]Western Literacy (Age 2)[/b] — +2 peasants/laborers, +5 soldiers (Western Europe)
[*] [b]Western Literacy (Age 4)[/b] — +5 burghers/soldiers, +3 peasants/laborers (Western Europe)
[*] [b]Colonial Literacy (Age 4)[/b] — +2 burghers/soldiers (Colonial powers)
[*] [b]East Asian Literacy (Age 4)[/b] — +5 nobles/burghers (East Asia)
[/list]

[hr][/hr]

[i]Report bugs on the Steam Workshop discussion page or the mod's GitHub repository.[/i]
